Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus protocol client (TCP read/write registers)
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, connect, read_holding_registers, write_register, isError, registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : minimalmodbus is serial (RTU/ASCII) focused and lacks a native TCP client class equivalent to ModbusTcpClient, so a TCP-based deployment 
    │   may require a serial gateway or protocol converter; Error handling differs: pymodbus returns response objects with isError(), while minimalmodbus and 
    │   modbus-tk typically raise exceptions on failure -- requires rewriting error-checking logic; Register addressing and unit/slave-id parameter conventions
    │   differ across libraries (e.g., 'unit=' kwarg vs positional slave address); Connection lifecycle management (connect/close) differs between libraries; 
    │   modbus-tk has a less actively maintained codebase and different object model for building requests
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries provide Modbus master/client functionality to read and write holding registers over a physical or network link, which is the exact 
        role pymodbus plays here (ModbusTcpClient, read_holding_registers, write_register). modbus-tk supports Modbus TCP similarly to pymodbus and could serve
        as a drop-in with an adapter layer. minimalmodbus is explicitly called out in the file's own docstring as a substitution target, though it is primarily
        designed for serial (RS-485/RS-232) communication rather than TCP, making it a weaker but still plausible alternative if a serial gateway or 
        TCP-to-serial bridge is used.

